WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:09.130
Germany is not only a very wealthy country,
it's also a country famous for running like

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:12.140
clockwork and for producing things of very
high quality.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:18.380
[That at least is the reputation, if it’s
German, it's good].

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.850
The fact is that over recent decades Germany
experienced one of the most significant economic

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.020
miracles in history.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.550
We have already told you about it both here
on VisualPolitik and on our sister channel

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:30.550
VisualEconomik.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:36.040
Today, Germany is synonymous with high productivity,
thriving companies and highly skilled workers.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.570
However, it has one problem, a potentially
very serious problem that we have not yet

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:43.710
told you about: its inability to attract skilled
labor.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.640
Have you ever considered going to work in
Germany?

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:51.210
I'm sure many of you have, but I'm sure many
more of you have thought about the UK, the

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.980
Netherlands, Canada, Australia or the US first.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.180
Despite being a very rich country, with many
jobs and what appears to be the opportunity

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.400
to build great careers, Germany does not seem
to be an attractive place for highly skilled

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.400
workers.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:07.080
It ranks much worse than the rest of its comparable
countries.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.260
It does not reach the extreme of Japan...

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:10.549
But it is an anomaly.

00:01:10.549 --> 00:01:14.869
An anomaly that would always be a problem,
but even more so when your working-age population

00:01:14.869 --> 00:01:15.870
is shrinking.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:20.330
So the question is, why on earth is Germany
such an unattractive destination for highly

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:21.590
qualified foreigners?

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:25.340
What could this mean for what has traditionally
been considered the driver of Europe?

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.510
What is the government doing to remedy this
situation?

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.630
Well, in this video we are going to answer
all these questions.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.369
And who knows, maybe by the end of it you
will consider moving there...

00:01:33.369 --> 00:01:35.000
Or maybe not.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:43.480
Let's get started.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.240
VisualPolitik viewers, Germany is a top 10
country.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.729
And by that I mean that it is almost always
among the top 10 countries when we look at

00:01:50.729 --> 00:01:57.159
practically any index of well-being and development:
GDP per capita, education levels, productivity

00:01:57.159 --> 00:01:59.969
levels, access to basic services, etc., etcetera.

00:01:59.969 --> 00:02:04.649
But its state-of-the-art factories, its modern
educational classrooms and its more than enviable

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:09.840
annual investment in techno-industrial development
would be meaningless if there were no teachers,

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.780
engineers, doctors, workers and professionals
to put this immense productive apparatus into

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:15.780
operation.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:16.780
And the problem...

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:22.959
We can start to see the problem perfectly
in this graph:

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:27.780
According to official UN figures, the German
population will start to shrink as of 2023.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.370
From a total population of around 83 million
people that it has at present, the forecast

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:37.900
is for the 80 million mark to be breached
by 2047 and to dip into the 70 millions by

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.330
the last decade of this century.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:44.590
It is not as dramatic a contraction as is
expected in countries such as Japan or South

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:46.920
Korea, but it is still economically relevant.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:52.319
This data basically means not only less population,
but also an aging population and, therefore,

00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:55.950
much less available labor.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:03:02.120
Logically, this is all in response to changes
in birth patterns, but there is something

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:07.070
else that can also increase the economic impact
of this decline: the lack of talent retention.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:10.250
Yes, Germany receives a lot of immigration,
that's for sure.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.250
It’s likely many of you have heard that.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:15.849
However, there is a problem in this story:
every time a highly skilled German worker

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:20.489
retires, it is not easy to replace him or
her, because as we will see, in Germany there

00:03:20.489 --> 00:03:25.610
is no surplus of immigration, but above all
there is no surplus of skilled immigration.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.990
So the question is: Who could say no to the
German giant?

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:30.959
Why are other destinations preferred?

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:33.250
These are the questions we have to find answers
to.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:42.610
In order to do so, the first thing of all
has to be to understand how we got here.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:48.230
THE FIRST DEMOGRAPHIC CHIMES

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:52.770
This is not an isolated case, most developed
countries – and also other not so developed

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:57.930
countries such as China and Russia – are
experiencing an accelerated process of demographic

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:58.930
aging.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:02.900
And, take note, because this process not only
reduces the available labor force, but also

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.860
considerably increases public spending:

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.459
Think about it, pensions, health care, dependence...

00:04:07.459 --> 00:04:10.299
The fact is that Germany understood the problem,
and guess what?

00:04:10.299 --> 00:04:14.709
It started to do its homework very early,
no less than half a century ago.

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:21.449
You see, in the 1970s there was a turning
point in Germany, its native population began

00:04:21.449 --> 00:04:22.449
to shrink.

00:04:22.449 --> 00:04:25.720
That is, the country entered a phase of negative
population growth.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:29.800
As you can see, since the 1970s the number
of deaths has exceeded the number of births

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:30.800
every year.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.270
To compensate for this, Germany started to
boost immigration.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:37.830
As you can see, the number of immigrants arriving
each year is generally greater than the native

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:39.310
loss of inhabitants.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:44.310
This has allowed the country to maintain its
population growth over the years.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.180
Everything depends on the arrival of new immigrants.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.690
And if you look at the graph you will see
how at the beginning of the 21st century the

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:53.910
first alarm was sounded: the arrival of immigrants
plummeted.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:58.639
At the beginning of the 21st century, almost
all of Europe was growing and growing faster

00:04:58.639 --> 00:04:59.639
than Germany.

00:04:59.639 --> 00:05:04.370
Germany was far from being the only flower
in an ever-expanding garden of prosperity...

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.720
And that, naturally, had consequences.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.289
Competition slowed down the influx of new
labor into German industries.

00:05:10.289 --> 00:05:12.480
The question is, what did they do?

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.780
The truth is that the Germans took it very,
very seriously.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:19.830
The government knew how important it was to
attract young talent and maintain demographic

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:24.009
expansion in order to prevent the economy
and the Social Security system from going

00:05:24.009 --> 00:05:25.009
into crisis.

00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:29.120
So to achieve their goals, in addition to
actively promoting immigration, they also

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.720
did things like this, check it out.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:35.039
(1 January 2005: Germany's first immigration
law goes into effect.

00:05:35.039 --> 00:05:38.660
Starting next year, new immigrants will be
entitled to participate in state-funded German

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:42.500
language classes and receive an introduction
to the country's legal system, culture and

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:43.500
history.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.020
The government has earmarked more than 200
million euros for the scheme.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:47.020
– DW)

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:53.840
The measures worked, and the net number of
immigrants arriving in the country each year

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:54.910
almost tripled.

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:59.669
From 73,000 in 2004, the number increased
to 212,000 in 2008.

00:05:59.669 --> 00:06:01.669
And not only that.

00:06:01.669 --> 00:06:07.970
By 2016, one of the historical ceilings in
terms of immigrant population was hit: 432,000

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:10.360
non-Germans moved to live in this country.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:14.130
Of course, Germany is part of the European
Union, so what does that mean?

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.699
That the country is subject to free mobility
of people and workers.

00:06:17.699 --> 00:06:20.520
Any of the EU members can become a rival country
for labor.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:27.020
A growing competition that Germany does not
seem to have been able to manage very well.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:31.880
"DANKE" BUT NO "DANKE")

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:39.340
From 2013 onwards, the influx of immigrants
to Germany started to slow down again.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:44.500
And even worse, according to OECD reports
the country was becoming less and less attractive

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.110
for the arrival of professionals.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:49.490
Because, and this is important, not all people
are equal.

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:52.570
Logically, from an economic point of view,
qualifications count.

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:55.600
And that is why the OECD issued this warning.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:56.600
Look at this.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:00.350
(4 February 2013: Labor migration: Germany
is open to graduates, but immigration is difficult

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:02.129
for medium-skilled workers).

00:07:02.129 --> 00:07:04.370
And why did the OECD say this?

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:05.370
Basically because of this.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:07.280
Pay attention to the data.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:11.620
("Germany is one of the OECD countries with
the lowest barriers to immigration for highly

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:12.620
skilled workers.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:16.770
However, the number of immigrant workers from
outside the EU and the European Free Trade

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:22.759
Area (EFTA) it receives is 25,000 per year,
about 0.02% of the population (...) The rate

00:07:22.759 --> 00:07:27.210
of skilled immigrant workers received by Australia,
Denmark, Canada and the United Kingdom is

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:29.149
five to ten times higher."

00:07:29.149 --> 00:07:31.150
– OECD German Labor Market Report.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:32.150
2013)

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:37.030
In other words, it was supposedly relatively
easy to emigrate to Germany, but even so,

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:40.310
skilled workers preferred – and still prefer
– other destinations.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:44.150
Whether because of the language, the standards
or the lifestyle, the fact is that Germany

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:49.770
is not a popular destination.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.540
Of course, that's not the whole story.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:56.190
A survey conducted by the Swiss-based International
Institute for Management Development found

00:07:56.190 --> 00:08:00.400
that German employers' perception of the ease
of recruiting international talent was rather

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.400
poor.

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:05.510
In other words, the OECD report did not accurately
reflect the entire reality.

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:08.590
It seems that red tape has been a problem
for a decade now.

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:13.509
In some ways, legislation to attract skilled
immigration dating back to 2005 was becoming

00:08:13.509 --> 00:08:16.979
obsolete compared to the incentives on the
table in neighboring countries.

00:08:16.979 --> 00:08:21.710
Therefore, between 2018 and 2020, the German
parliament started to make several changes

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:22.710
to the Act.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:25.960
Changes to make it easier for professionals
from outside the European Union.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:29.639
For example, it was made easier for immigrants
to arrive without a contract.

00:08:29.639 --> 00:08:33.210
However, just then, the pandemic completely
changed the picture.

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:36.640
It brought the arrival of new immigrants to
a screeching halt.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:38.800
Immigrants of all kinds: skilled and unskilled.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:43.200
And that, in the long run, only aggravated
the problems.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:50.460
Since 2013, the Federal Labor Agency itself
has been monitoring what the authorities refer

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:52.680
to as "bottleneck" occupations.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:57.650
That is, areas of the labor market where job
vacancies cannot be filled by available workers.

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:01.980
In 2020 this situation cooled down, but since
then the trend has returned.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:05.600
More and more areas of the German economy
are unable to get the skilled workers they

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:06.600
need.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:13.140
In fact, this indicator is expected to break
records in 2023.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:17.690
The post-pandemic economic upturn coupled
with the growing demographic aging is pushing

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:21.540
the German labor market into an increasingly
critical context:

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:25.040
While the demand for workers continues to
grow, the number of immigrants arriving each

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:26.610
year has decreased.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:28.260
Not to mention the number of skilled immigrants.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:32.329
To give you an idea of what we are talking
about, according to estimates by the German

00:09:32.329 --> 00:09:38.410
Economic Institute in Cologne, by mid-2022
there were already 1.5 million vacancies in

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:39.410
this country.

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:42.019
A figure that is expected to grow strongly
over the next few years.

00:09:42.019 --> 00:09:47.050
The situation has reached such an extent that
some commercial areas have had to start providing

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:49.560
their services on a reduced basis.

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:52.480
And this is, in fact, what explains news stories
like this:

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:56.420
(25 June 2022: Germany will use temporary
foreign workers to ease staff shortages at

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:57.420
German airports.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:01.490
Photos of travelers standing in long lines
at security checkpoints at Düsseldorf airport

00:10:01.490 --> 00:10:03.750
earlier this week showed the extent of the
shortage.

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:04.750
– Reuters)

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:08.399
In other words, the German economy is in urgent
need of labor of all kinds.

00:10:08.399 --> 00:10:12.500
According to estimates by the German Institute
for Labor Market and Employment Research,

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:21.350
at least 400,000 new arrivals are needed each
year to keep the workforce stable.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:22.350
That's already a problem.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:25.270
But, VisualPolitik viewers, the free skilled
labor thing...

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:28.279
That's already almost an endangered species
in this country.

00:10:28.279 --> 00:10:33.550
According to a survey conducted in December
2022, 7 out of 10 companies are short of skilled

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:34.550
labor.

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:36.900
Not only that, but Germany is now facing another
problem:

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:40.440
It's no longer that few skilled workers are
arriving, it's that many of the ones they

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:42.410
did have are leaving.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:45.010
In other words, the country is suffering a
brain drain.

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:49.589
And that, that is something that endangers
the formidable industry that fuels Europe's

00:10:49.589 --> 00:10:50.920
greatest economic engine.

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:51.920
But...

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:54.279
Let's go back to the question we asked at
the beginning: How on earth is it possible

00:10:54.279 --> 00:10:58.589
that there are almost no professionals who
want to live and work in one of the most developed

00:10:58.589 --> 00:10:59.930
countries in the world?

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:02.389
Where is the problem?

00:11:02.389 --> 00:11:04.010
Well...

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:07.250
Listen up!

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:12.900
THE THREE PILLARS OF THE TALENT DRAIN)

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:16.860
If we try to go straight to the heart of the
matter, we could say that Germany faces three

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:17.860
major problems.

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:19.470
Each of them linked to the others.

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:20.710
For example, first of all...

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:28.149
The stock of human capital from Europe cannot
be expected to last forever!

00:11:28.149 --> 00:11:29.420
And why do I say this?

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:34.690
Well, you see, according to official figures,
Serbia has lost 9% of its inhabitants in the

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:42.310
last three decades, North Macedonia 10%, Bosnia
and Herzegovina 24% and Albania 37%.

00:11:42.310 --> 00:11:46.490
And something very similar could be said about
Poland and the Baltic republics, among others.

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:50.460
All these countries have lost a large proportion
of their best-educated and most qualified

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:51.460
young people.

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:55.840
And, let’s not forget, Europe is a very
aging continent, so the pool of skilled workers

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:58.920
available for German companies to recruit
is limited.

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:06.740
Basically, the pool of skilled workers is
no longer large enough.

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:10.830
Along these lines, many experts believe that
the "initial effect" of the EU expansions

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:12.459
has already come to an end.

00:12:12.459 --> 00:12:17.019
Perhaps Ukraine was the only exception, but
this country has also already suffered a huge

00:12:17.019 --> 00:12:20.080
exodus of skilled workers, particularly of
young people.

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:25.149
For this reason, many analysts point out that
the search for talent must look beyond continental

00:12:25.149 --> 00:12:26.149
borders.

00:12:26.149 --> 00:12:27.149
And do you know what?

00:12:27.149 --> 00:12:31.199
This is exactly what led the 2020 reform of
the Skilled Immigration Act to introduce more

00:12:31.199 --> 00:12:34.190
facilities for skilled immigrants from third
countries.

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:38.690
For example, university degrees were no longer
required, but vocational training was also

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:40.660
accepted as proof of qualification.

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:44.150
A demand that German employers have been making
for some time.

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:45.150
Why?

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:47.820
Just take a look at this graph.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:49.800
Of course, this was not the only measure.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:53.660
Some administrative formalities were also
reduced, but the main stumbling block was

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:54.660
not solved.

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:59.519
Which is none other than this.

00:12:59.519 --> 00:13:04.140
The bureaucratic German immigration system
is extremely complex: at least 7 ministries

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:08.100
are involved in the process, without taking
into account second-tier government agencies

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:09.100
and local governments.

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:13.160
To give just one example, the approval of
a qualified professional would involve:

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:16.410
the Federal Ministry of Economic Affairs and
Climate Action,

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:20.699
the Ministry of Labor and Social Affairs,
the Ministry of Education and Research,

00:13:20.699 --> 00:13:23.700
the Ministry of Food and Agriculture,
the Ministry of Health,

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:28.279
the Ministry of the Interior and Community
and the Ministry of Economic Cooperation and

00:13:28.279 --> 00:13:34.290
Development – so pretty straightforward!

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:37.709
This obviously poses a major disincentive
for German companies.

00:13:37.709 --> 00:13:43.600
In 2022, only 17% of the companies surveyed
by the Bertels-mann Foundation recruited skilled

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:44.600
workers from abroad.

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:49.600
And while large companies can hire consultancies
and law firms to speed up the process, SMEs

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:51.690
face an almost insurmountable barrier.

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:56.820
That is, in a way, the 2020 eligibility extension
was a step in the right direction to start

00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:00.850
attracting professionals from outside the
European Union, however, it did not prepare

00:14:00.850 --> 00:14:03.630
the bureaucracy for such a challenge:

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:07.180
Mobility within the common European block
is much simpler, but when you start incorporating

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:11.480
professionals from other regions, the documentation
and requirements multiply.

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:16.430
It is infinitely more difficult and costly
to incorporate a Thai or Uruguayan worker

00:14:16.430 --> 00:14:17.470
than a Polish one.

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:19.040
This is the second problem.

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:23.279
And finally, the third major stumbling block
has to do with a much more complex factor:

00:14:23.279 --> 00:14:24.290
the cultural one...

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:29.810
If only because of the language.

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:34.949
In August 2022, the German Federal Employment
Agency turned its attention to this issue.

00:14:34.949 --> 00:14:35.949
And do you know what it found?

00:14:35.949 --> 00:14:40.430
That most workers who arrive in Germany without
knowing German do not even bother to try to

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:41.430
learn it.

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:42.430
Why?

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:43.730
Because they are thinking of leaving.

00:14:43.730 --> 00:14:48.230
Language integration problems, problems in
obtaining permanent residence, housing costs,

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:52.839
cost of living and taxes, problems in reuniting
the family in the country and the clash with

00:14:52.839 --> 00:14:57.320
the local culture are the most frequent arguments
given by skilled workers who decide to leave

00:14:57.320 --> 00:14:58.320
Germany.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:01.750
This is something we have already told you
about on VisualEconomik.

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:06.360
To make matters worse, the immigration legislation
clashes with a new type of work that is practically

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:07.699
ignored: freelancers.

00:15:07.699 --> 00:15:11.970
Well, these three problems seem to explain
why Germany is not as attractive a country

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:13.730
as it apparently should be.

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:15.589
The question is, is there a way out?

00:15:15.589 --> 00:15:17.060
What are German politicians doing?

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:23.660
Well, we are going to take a look at that
right now.

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:29.470
RETAKING THE "JOB APPEAL"

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:33.750
Estimates by the German Ministry of Labor
suggest that, if this situation is not reversed

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:39.149
by 2026, more than 200,000 jobs currently
filled will become vacant.

00:15:39.149 --> 00:15:43.660
A situation that could have a very negative
impact on companies, productivity and the

00:15:43.660 --> 00:15:44.910
country's economic growth.

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:48.660
Therefore, in October 2022, the issue will
be at the top of the public agenda:

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:52.350
(22 October 2022: How Germany plans to attract
qualified foreigners.

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:56.319
Reforms include the introduction of a so-called
"opportunity card" that will entitle people

00:15:56.319 --> 00:16:01.130
to seek work in Germany following a point
system based on language skills, professional

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:03.769
experience and connection to Germany, among
other criteria).

00:16:03.769 --> 00:16:07.779
And finally, on 29 March, the Qualified Immigration
Law was amended again,

00:16:07.779 --> 00:16:11.780
(29 March 2023: Foreigners with adequate work
experience and education in their home country

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:15.240
will be allowed to work in Germany, even if
those professional qualifications are not

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:16.529
recognized in Germany.

00:16:16.529 --> 00:16:19.620
In addition, they will be allowed to work
up to 20 hours per week while looking for

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:20.620
long-term employment.

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:24.660
Finally, it will be possible for persons in
possession of academic degrees or professional

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:28.300
certificates to remain in Germany for up to
one year while seeking employment.

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:29.300
– DW)

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:30.300
Will this new reform work?

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:31.300
Only time will tell.

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:32.420
The question now is:

00:16:32.420 --> 00:16:34.300
Would you consider living in Germany?

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:35.300
Would you like to?

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:38.040
How do you think this country can boost the
arrival of new talent?

00:16:38.040 --> 00:16:40.889
Leave us your answer in the comments and let's
open a debate.

00:16:40.889 --> 00:16:44.009
And now, if you found this video interesting,
don't forget to give it a like and subscribe

00:16:44.009 --> 00:16:46.680
to VisualPolitik if you haven't already done
so.

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:47.839
All the best and see you next time.

